  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism of action linking the compound 2-[[(2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxymethyl]-2H-furan-5-one to the LS174T cell line. This compound is identified as a target of multiple drugs, including Leosibirone B, Oxyberberrubine, and 7-Hydroxy-10-ethoxydehydrodihydrobotrydial, all of which are directed toward the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. This subunit is a known target of several compounds, and its inhibition is associated with the suppression of LS174T cell growth. The evidence suggests that the compound may act through a proteasome-dependent mechanism, as it is targeted by multiple drugs that inhibit the proteasome. The final score indicates a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, supported by the shared target of multiple drugs and the known role of the proteasome in cell cycle regulation. The mechanism is supported by the presence of a common target and the involvement of the proteasome, which is a well-characterized pathway in cellular processes.",
